 do with  food race this is if you've got a bigger  plate you don't put as much on it if you  can't carry as much you don't bring more  than you can fit in your mouth these are  the dining halls yeah yeah that's right  the dining halls so i mean there's all  sorts of different approaches you can  take both from like the value of food  altogether to some of these like very  specific institutional interventions of  sort of saying okay what the system  right now is encouraging people to keep  wasting things interesting so that  reminds me the yale sustainable food  project is is a lot more than than the  yale farm oh yes so uh talk about just  some of the other major projects or  elements of the of the food project sure  the yale sustainable food project  although many people think of it as the  ale farm is a global thing so we work on  the farm both on our main campus as well  as well as the new west campus farm we  work in the classrooms and those are in  about 40 courses on the calendar each  year and they span every discipline you  can imagine food through an economics  lens food through a history lens through  an anthropology lens and then  increasingly we're working around the  world  and the reason for that is because right  to the beginning question  the sustainable farm  in one part of the world is not going to  be the same as a sustainable farm  somewhere else in the world  and so we can teach the principles of  sustainable agriculture the yale farm  but we can't teach the examples of  context-specific sustainable agriculture  without our students getting out in the  world  getting their boots on the ground and  seeing it firsthand  so the students who  start to learn about food at the yale  farm  increasingly and we hope it's going to  be up to 100 in a year they're going off  to farms around the world and learning  about okay what are the uh you know what  are the challenges what are the  opportunities and it's not just farming  because farming is the heart and soul of  the food system but it's not the food  system so our students are also going  off to businesses who are working on say  distribution marketing preparation maybe  the food distribution businesses maybe  the restaurants they're also working in  the health disciplines because  ultimately understanding how the food we  put in our bodies impacts our health is  one of these  big questions right now which is it's a  major missing link in the picture of how  we link the way we use the planet  to the way that people ultimately are  healthy and have good lives and so our  students  are going around the world to gain  experience in all of those different  subsystems of the larger food system  and learning about how it changes  everywhere you are in the world when  you've got a different cultural setting  a different biophysical setting how do  we have to approach this problem  differently when we're somewhere else in  the world  and so the sustainable food project is  global today and necessarily so because  the problems of yale and connecticut are  not the problems of the world  and yale students we produce some very  good leaders who i think understand uh  how to make policy changes how to make  financial changes how to look at the  systematic and systemic uh root causes  of the problem and figure out how to  tackle that big problem and to do that  they need a global perspective so we can  offer that  we've got just about 30 seconds for a  minute left let me ask you about some of  the some of the other ways that you  personally sort of practice agricultural  environmental sustainability  sustainability in your own life okay  um this is kind of a tough one because  probably the single most important thing  i could do if i was thinking about still  working in this country would be to move  to new york  and the reason for that is because in  this context it's actually living in  dense urban environments where you're  not heating a large space  where you don't have to rely on vast  distances to get to and from work and  all the amenities you need to go to  it's living small and local in that way  is the most important personal choice  you can make for sustainability  and so what i can do living in yale and  new haven is try and make new haven as  much like new york as i possibly can so  you know i bike here this morning  i will be biking off to try and eat at  some local restaurant or local coffee  shop after this interview and  working basically to embed the local  businesses the local farms  into all of my daily life like basically  trying to keep it local means that i  have to move around less  and living in a place which is close by  and is compact and enjoyable means i  have to eat less  and then of course you know i try and  watch food waste i try and eat foods  that are actually going to be  that i know are going to be delicious  too delicious to throw away and  i try and make meat the kind of  sought-after small pleasure not the  center of the plate but rather that uh  i guess kind of accompaniment to a plate  which is heavy in delicious plant-based  food  i'm going to try to sneak in one more  question that we got from twitter  by uh lorenzo wy which maybe stands for  wyoming i don't know  how would you encourage or compel  uh big agricultural companies like  monsanto to make their methods more  sustainable  oh that's a really interesting question  and actually i've been encouraged  because some of yale's graduates  who are deeply concerned about food  sustainability just passionate about it  have actually ended up working for very  very large firms and are now taking  roles within those firms as say chief  sustainability officers  or new high-ranking positions that  companies are opening up to give  sustainability leadership in that firm  and i actually think there is potential  for considerable transformation in some  of these big companies the the big like  possibility  is you look at the size of the supply  chains some of these companies take  walmart for an example and you think wow  if we could just just get  some internal changes  it would  resonate throughout a supply chain that  is bigger than most countries  and so something small from the inside  could end up being something very very  big on the outside so i do think there's  some potential there and i do think  actually by thinking of all the players  in the food system rather than focusing  on a subset uh we'll have a better  chance at actually achieving the kind of  sustainability we need so you so you you  would affect change or think it's most  likely to happen  effectively by getting people who have  who have absorbed and appreciate  sustainable food practices working  inside these companies yeah we say that  our product if you like is uh food  literate leaders  and the idea is that we need people  working on all fronts of the food system  inside big companies inside small  companies different approaches because  we have different challenges mark  bonford director of the yale sustainable  food project thank you very much for  joining us today and thanks to all of  you for watching at yale live please do  join us again next month  you

